00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:11.270
like more than 10 years
ago-- so a long history

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:14.900
of really iterating,
trying, and failing,

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:18.800
but exploring the best
way to run application

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:20.750
with the proper isolation.

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:24.260
So let's rewind the
clock a little bit

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:28.860
and see what we have
done in the past.

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:32.230
So take one, run as root.

00:05:32.230 --> 00:05:33.590
This is a very bold statement.

00:05:33.590 --> 00:05:36.290
I still feel kind of
nervous to say so.

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:39.579
I checked with my tech
leads a couple of times.

00:05:39.579 --> 00:05:40.120
Is this real?

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:42.650
Like, did you really,
guys, run things at root?

00:05:42.650 --> 00:05:44.390
And the answer was yes.

00:05:44.390 --> 00:05:48.920
And apparently nothing
shields the system.

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:52.150
Your process can basically
access all devices there.

00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:54.830
Of course, you can
access the whole file--

00:05:54.830 --> 00:05:56.990
the host file system.

00:05:56.990 --> 00:05:59.030
All resources available for you.

00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:02.330
You can configure the
network as you like,

00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:05.180
and you can perform any
system calls, of course.

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:09.240
And even you can send
sick kill to others.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:11.495
So it's pretty, pretty--

00:06:11.495 --> 00:06:13.370
I mean, when I heard
about this, I just went,

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:15.860
like, I'm pretty impressed
in a different way.

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:18.400
And of course what
if it goes wrong?

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:19.280
Of course, right?

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:23.030
And this rm, rf, and star--

00:06:23.030 --> 00:06:25.430
still this kind of a
sequence of characters

00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:26.480
make me nervous, right?

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:28.976
I'm pretty sure that
everyone in this room

00:06:28.976 --> 00:06:30.350
has kind of
experienced this once

00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:33.560
or twice in their whole
career and kind of regret it.

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:35.132
Your data is gone of course.

00:06:35.132 --> 00:06:36.840
But more seriously,
that missed operation

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:40.730
is a real thing, but more into
it from a security context,

00:06:40.730 --> 00:06:45.110
what if the malicious software
took advantage of this?

00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:46.870
And basically there's no answer.

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:51.340
So take two.

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:52.620
Run as unprivileged user.

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:55.420
Right, yeah, this you
should do by default.

00:06:55.420 --> 00:06:59.050
So basically give away
the privilege of root.

00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:01.630
You don't need it.

00:07:01.630 --> 00:07:05.310
You have only limited
access to those devices,

00:07:05.310 --> 00:07:08.260
including network devices.

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:12.280
File system, of course,
will be limited,

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:14.860
and what kind of
system calls that you

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:20.530
can call will be also
checked, and blocked if not.

00:07:20.530 --> 00:07:22.690
And also the sending
a signal capability

00:07:22.690 --> 00:07:25.400
will be also limited.

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:26.950
So great, all
right, good enough.

00:07:26.950 --> 00:07:32.080
But what if the process
can have a capability

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:37.900
to set the PID, or you
could create a raw socket.

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:41.210
You know, you can still
do something there.

00:07:41.210 --> 00:07:43.840
So now take three.

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:46.240
Dropping capabilities.

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:50.590
Linux has a function
called capabilities,

00:07:50.590 --> 00:07:54.350
which would split the
capabilities of that as

00:07:54.350 --> 00:07:57.649
aggregated to root into
a much more finer ring.

00:07:57.649 --> 00:07:59.440
You know, basically
with fewer capabilities

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:03.550
than basically you have
a better isolation there.

00:08:03.550 --> 00:08:05.690
So for example, like
loading a kernel module,

00:08:05.690 --> 00:08:11.770
which is apparently a big thing,
admin capability, capability

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:15.340
to setting the system
clock, and networking,

00:08:15.340 --> 00:08:20.110
and the list goes
on and on and on.

00:08:20.110 --> 00:08:22.610
So, OK, now we have
the capability,

00:08:22.610 --> 00:08:27.190
so we somehow manage to
set what a process can do,

00:08:27.190 --> 00:08:30.010
and what a process cannot do.

00:08:30.010 --> 00:08:33.460
Well what if a process can
consume all the resources?

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:35.520
What about the
resource isolation?

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:38.610
We don't have anything.

00:08:38.610 --> 00:08:40.770
So take four.

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:42.260
Cgroups.

00:08:42.260 --> 00:08:47.180
So to address the resource
isolation problem,

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:53.120
Google has in the past
contributed this control group,

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:55.820
which will allow
limiting the resources

00:08:55.820 --> 00:08:59.360
and accounting them
for better isolation.

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:03.170
For example, it will
limit access to the CPUs,

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:08.540
and it will also account the CPU
usage by the particular cgroup.

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:14.480
And same for a limit, and
account for the memory usage.

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:17.150
You could even assign
cores and memories--

00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:23.540
memory nodes-- to
any particular group,

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:27.080
and also you can also control
the access to the device

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:30.580
based on which cgroup
that you're in.

00:09:30.580 --> 00:09:36.860
Great, now we figured out
what the process can do when

00:09:36.860 --> 00:09:39.020
your resources will be limited.

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:42.020
But still, a process
can see others.

00:09:42.020 --> 00:09:45.800
It can still see what
others are doing.

00:09:45.800 --> 00:09:47.510
Take five.

00:09:47.510 --> 00:09:49.074
Namespace.

00:09:49.074 --> 00:09:50.990
I actually didn't know,
but namespace actually

00:09:50.990 --> 00:09:52.360
existed a long time ago.

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:54.840
And yesterday I was
looking into it.

00:09:54.840 --> 00:09:57.180
And in past, when the
cgroup was proposed,

00:09:57.180 --> 00:10:00.830
this integration with namespace
happened at that time.

00:10:00.830 --> 00:10:02.990
So namespace
basically provides you

00:10:02.990 --> 00:10:10.610
that visibility-- that isolation
for each namesake's type.

00:10:10.610 --> 00:10:13.560
And for example,
like the networking,

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:20.010
PID, mount point, user,
IPC, a lot of things.

00:10:20.010 --> 00:10:24.950
So with this namespace and
also the resource limitation,

00:10:24.950 --> 00:10:28.190
your resource
isolation with cgroups,

00:10:28.190 --> 00:10:30.800
it's a huge improvement.

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:32.386
I think it took quite a bit.

00:10:32.386 --> 00:10:34.760
Like, I'm pretty sure it was
like two or three years when

00:10:34.760 --> 00:10:39.020
I started seeing the proposal
from Google back in 2007-ish,

00:10:39.020 --> 00:10:40.840
I think.

00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:43.520
So it's a huge, huge
improvement compared

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:47.580
to what Linux was at that time.

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:49.730
I'm not sure how it's
doing today, but you know,

00:10:49.730 --> 00:10:52.220
the original concept was
more from the [INAUDIBLE],,

00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:55.820
but I was always told that Linux
doesn't have that capability.

00:10:55.820 --> 00:10:59.130
But at this point I think it
was very clear that now there

00:10:59.130 --> 00:11:02.580
was a so-called container
capability inside Linux,

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:03.680
so it was great.

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:09.710
And I hope I'm done with talking
about this, but life is unfair.

00:11:09.710 --> 00:11:15.170
Someone said a very
inconvenient truth.

00:11:15.170 --> 00:11:17.230
Containers do not contain.

00:11:17.230 --> 00:11:20.390
It's a very short
word, but it's so true.

00:11:20.390 --> 00:11:22.310
It points out the
fundamental part

00:11:22.310 --> 00:11:26.140
that we all kind talk about.

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:27.710
The statement is
really so powerful

00:11:27.710 --> 00:11:32.240
that it reveals a fundamental
challenge about isolating

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:35.140
processes in containers.

00:11:35.140 --> 00:11:35.990
But you might ask--

00:11:35.990 --> 00:11:41.450
I think I also wasn't paying so
much attention until I realized

00:11:41.450 --> 00:11:44.990
this point, but OK, but you
have some sort of isolation

00:11:44.990 --> 00:11:48.500
from a visibility capability
and resource consumption.

00:11:48.500 --> 00:11:54.430
Why are we so focused about
these containers not contain?

00:11:54.430 --> 00:11:55.805
And I think it's
a good question.

00:11:58.770 --> 00:12:02.000
So we're focused
about on containers

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:06.500
not to contain because
what we're discussing here

00:12:06.500 --> 00:12:11.830
is about the threats from the
inside of those containers.

00:12:11.830 --> 00:12:14.450
And that would, then,
like this picture--

00:12:14.450 --> 00:12:17.990
we're assuming this cute
cat is the attacker,

00:12:17.990 --> 00:12:21.200
and the victim are
the parrots there.

00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:24.590
Anyone wonder, who actually
put that cat inside the cage?

00:12:24.590 --> 00:12:27.690
Who actually put a threat
inside a container?

00:12:27.690 --> 00:12:29.960
And yes, understanding
that threat model

00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:31.650
is very, very important.

00:12:31.650 --> 00:12:32.959
There are two major cases.

00:12:32.959 --> 00:12:35.250
I mean, I think there are
more detailed cases, but just

00:12:35.250 --> 00:12:38.820
very roughly, two types.

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:42.980
One is, if you're
running a service that

00:12:42.980 --> 00:12:48.680
allows users to submit code,
like SAAS and multi-tenancy,

00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:54.335
like for us perhaps a Google App
Engine could be a good example.

00:12:54.335 --> 00:12:57.650
A very obvious case I
think that I'm aware of,

00:12:57.650 --> 00:13:00.230
if you're running a
WordPress and perhaps

00:13:00.230 --> 00:13:03.940
allow users to use
their favorite plug-in,

00:13:03.940 --> 00:13:06.260
or their own customized
plug-in and there you go.

00:13:06.260 --> 00:13:10.700
You have a code that you
haven't been able to certify,

00:13:10.700 --> 00:13:14.570
and that's when one
particular problem--

00:13:14.570 --> 00:13:16.970
a particular case-- that
it could actually put

00:13:16.970 --> 00:13:19.540
the threat inside your system.

00:13:19.540 --> 00:13:22.650
The one which is somewhat
relevant is a security breach.

00:13:22.650 --> 00:13:23.450
Yes, attack.

00:13:23.450 --> 00:13:25.700
First of all, like a
firewall and everything,

00:13:25.700 --> 00:13:30.080
you would want to first
prevent any threat coming

00:13:30.080 --> 00:13:31.360
into your system.

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:34.460
However, we all know
that we do our best,

00:13:34.460 --> 00:13:36.170
but something happened.

00:13:36.170 --> 00:13:40.160
So once that incident
happens, the biggest principle

00:13:40.160 --> 00:13:42.320
that we have any Google
in terms of security

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:45.440
is that we have to have
multiple layers of security.

00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:49.520
So if you only have one bar,
then that's compromised,

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:55.560
and then it gets in, then
your entire system is at risk.

00:13:55.560 --> 00:13:57.662
So then if you have
an attacker coming

00:13:57.662 --> 00:13:59.120
into one of your
containers, if you

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:02.600
don't have anything
protecting or containing

00:14:02.600 --> 00:14:06.590
inside at that point, then
all of the sudden one attack

00:14:06.590 --> 00:14:08.570
could affect the rest.

00:14:08.570 --> 00:14:11.840
So those are things that
we wanted to be careful.

00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:13.470
And especially the latter case.

00:14:13.470 --> 00:14:17.780
If you're running a system for
a larger organization, right?

00:14:17.780 --> 00:14:20.420
If you have a full control
over what kind of code you go,

00:14:20.420 --> 00:14:23.390
I think your chances of getting
to the problem is lowered,

00:14:23.390 --> 00:14:26.630
but if you have a much
more distributed team

00:14:26.630 --> 00:14:28.790
with a lot of different
kind of background

00:14:28.790 --> 00:14:32.240
then you may or may not be
able to control that fully.

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:34.160
So if you want to be--

00:14:34.160 --> 00:14:37.220
if your want more scale or
agility, than you actually

00:14:37.220 --> 00:14:39.460
might want to think
about really containers

00:14:39.460 --> 00:14:41.330
to contain the threat inside.

00:14:45.850 --> 00:14:49.360
So when we talk about the threat
from the inside, what we're

00:14:49.360 --> 00:14:55.690
talking about is we're talking
about attack through the Linux

00:14:55.690 --> 00:15:02.590
kernel, which will allow these
containers to escape outside

00:15:02.590 --> 00:15:05.340
of the isolation that
we talked earlier.

00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:12.680
They usually exploit bugs inside
a Linux kernel through a system

00:15:12.680 --> 00:15:15.370
service, like the obvious
one, system calls,

00:15:15.370 --> 00:15:21.630
but like /proc files, and to
bypass all the long history

00:15:21.630 --> 00:15:26.110
of the addin mechanism inside
this kernel and bypass that,

00:15:26.110 --> 00:15:27.820
and eventually
elevate the privilege.

00:15:27.820 --> 00:15:32.110
So escape and then attack
the kernel, and then break it

00:15:32.110 --> 00:15:33.920
and take the privilege
of the system.

00:15:33.920 --> 00:15:36.410
So this is the kind of very
high level, it's more detailed,

00:15:36.410 --> 00:15:40.120
but that is the overall picture
of the threat at that time

00:15:40.120 --> 00:15:42.850
that we're talking about.

00:15:42.850 --> 00:15:45.010
So Linux kernel.

00:15:45.010 --> 00:15:47.860
You may not notice because
it's so widely used,

00:15:47.860 --> 00:15:50.060
and indeed it's great.

00:15:50.060 --> 00:15:52.510
It's a lot of people paying
attention to security.

00:15:52.510 --> 00:15:56.350
But reality is that it
has a huge attack surface.

00:15:56.350 --> 00:16:03.320
Just by numbers, there are more
than 300 system calls there.

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:08.380
There are more than 2,000
CVs reported since 1999, out

00:16:08.380 --> 00:16:13.150
of which more in 200 are
previous escalations.

00:16:13.150 --> 00:16:16.680
So I'm not trying to
actually really criticize

00:16:16.680 --> 00:16:19.510
about Linux kernel.

00:16:19.510 --> 00:16:22.870
Like I mentioned earlier,
if your project product

00:16:22.870 --> 00:16:26.860
code gets popular, then
two things happen, right?

00:16:26.860 --> 00:16:28.510
You add more features
that you have

00:16:28.510 --> 00:16:31.570
to respond to more of
those and functionality.

00:16:31.570 --> 00:16:34.330
Then the system gets
complex, and then

00:16:34.330 --> 00:16:37.750
if you get more popularity than
you have chances of attackers

00:16:37.750 --> 00:16:42.730
paying attention to attack
the product or project,

00:16:42.730 --> 00:16:45.055
because more audience
and they want

00:16:45.055 --> 00:16:47.430
it to be more effective when
they think about the attack.

00:16:50.710 --> 00:16:52.630
One of the most
famous ones, I think,

00:16:52.630 --> 00:16:55.900
of that pattern of
attack is Dirty COW.

00:16:55.900 --> 00:16:59.860
That happened to
a report in 2016.

00:16:59.860 --> 00:17:03.130
It's a privilege escalation of
vulnerability inside a Linux

00:17:03.130 --> 00:17:04.920
kernel.

00:17:04.920 --> 00:17:08.829
An unprivileged local user
could exploit a race condition

00:17:08.829 --> 00:17:11.940
the Copy On Write--
so therefore COW--

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:14.650
mechanism in the
Linux kernel subsystem

00:17:14.650 --> 00:17:18.220
to increase their
privilege on the system.

00:17:18.220 --> 00:17:22.609
So honestly, I was already
at Google, working at Google,

00:17:22.609 --> 00:17:26.319
and I was always arguing about,
so what is the right isolation

00:17:26.319 --> 00:17:27.490
for containers.

00:17:27.490 --> 00:17:30.280
And I was more in the
camp of, well, containers

00:17:30.280 --> 00:17:31.692
should be doing very well.

00:17:31.692 --> 00:17:33.900
If you [INAUDIBLE] Linux,
and if you have these runs,

00:17:33.900 --> 00:17:34.690
it should be good.

00:17:34.690 --> 00:17:36.539
But this Dirty COW came up.

00:17:36.539 --> 00:17:38.080
And it was a different
security team,

00:17:38.080 --> 00:17:40.420
but they mentioned that,
yeah, there was Dirty COW,

00:17:40.420 --> 00:17:44.320
and this one happened, and then
really opened up my eyes that,

00:17:44.320 --> 00:17:47.810
OK, so if it's a complex
system then literally

00:17:47.810 --> 00:17:54.400
we have to be prepared for
an unexpected situation.

00:17:54.400 --> 00:17:59.380
So to be fair, there
are tools for experts.

00:17:59.380 --> 00:18:03.580
I'll see why I say experts, but
one is SELinux, and AppArmor,

00:18:03.580 --> 00:18:07.660
and more, based on a Linux
security module, which

00:18:07.660 --> 00:18:10.120
is mandatory access
control which

00:18:10.120 --> 00:18:14.340
provides fine-grain access
control per process.

00:18:14.340 --> 00:18:19.390
So really you can describe
very well what a process can do

00:18:19.390 --> 00:18:21.610
and cannot do.

00:18:21.610 --> 00:18:24.010
The second one, Seccomp BPF.

00:18:24.010 --> 00:18:28.410
Seccomp is short for a Secure
Computing Mode inside Linux,

00:18:28.410 --> 00:18:32.200
and this dash BPF-- the
Berkeley Packet Filter--

00:18:32.200 --> 00:18:36.310
is a filler by itself, but
what it means by the second BPF

00:18:36.310 --> 00:18:39.580
is that you can actually
specify the policy

00:18:39.580 --> 00:18:42.430
by using language
coming with the BPF,

00:18:42.430 --> 00:18:44.060
and apply it to Seccomp.

00:18:44.060 --> 00:18:47.140
And in an extension
of that, you can

00:18:47.140 --> 00:18:54.500
define which syscall should be
filtered with the BPF rules.

00:18:54.500 --> 00:18:56.596
Just to kind of gave
an example, before

00:18:56.596 --> 00:18:57.970
coming to Kubernetes
Engine Team,

00:18:57.970 --> 00:19:00.670
I worked at Chrome Webs
Team, and I actually

00:19:00.670 --> 00:19:03.730
did bring Android
apps to Chrome Webs.

00:19:03.730 --> 00:19:08.890
So I had the luck to look into
both Chrome Webs and Android

00:19:08.890 --> 00:19:09.560
at same time.

00:19:09.560 --> 00:19:11.710
And it's interesting
to me, because Android

00:19:11.710 --> 00:19:15.950
used SELinux intensively,
and the Chrome Webs

00:19:15.950 --> 00:19:17.630
used Seccomps intensively.

00:19:17.630 --> 00:19:21.280
What it means is
SELinux in Android,

00:19:21.280 --> 00:19:23.960
working app compatibility
is very important.

00:19:23.960 --> 00:19:26.950
So you actually want to
have why syscall to cover,

00:19:26.950 --> 00:19:29.350
or the framework to cover.

00:19:29.350 --> 00:19:34.150
Therefore, you need to harden
the Linux kernel very strictly.

00:19:34.150 --> 00:19:37.810
And they even have a test
suite and also the policy

00:19:37.810 --> 00:19:42.310
that you have to get certified
to be named as Android.

00:19:42.310 --> 00:19:44.650
On the other hand,
Chrome Webs, they're

00:19:44.650 --> 00:19:49.810
using Seccomp BPF to sandbox
their Chrome browser rendering.

00:19:49.810 --> 00:19:53.650
And the philosophy there is
to reduce the attack surface,

00:19:53.650 --> 00:19:56.290
because there's only browser.

00:19:56.290 --> 00:19:58.150
The browser is in the
application framework,

00:19:58.150 --> 00:20:01.630
so all you want it to focus
is what the browser is

00:20:01.630 --> 00:20:04.030
going to send to this system.

00:20:04.030 --> 00:20:05.290
So, yeah, we have a system.

00:20:05.290 --> 00:20:10.630
And the question is,
are these tools for me?

00:20:10.630 --> 00:20:11.520
Maybe for you.

00:20:11.520 --> 00:20:14.590
If you do know the
policy very well,

00:20:14.590 --> 00:20:17.459
if you know what application
you want to pair-- like I said,

00:20:17.459 --> 00:20:19.250
in the case of Chrome,
it's just a browser.

00:20:19.250 --> 00:20:21.150
If you know, then it's great.

00:20:21.150 --> 00:20:25.450
But the problem is
that the more detail

00:20:25.450 --> 00:20:28.960
you describe in a policy,
the chances of that policy

00:20:28.960 --> 00:20:31.810
to be generic for
any other process

00:20:31.810 --> 00:20:33.610
will be lower, because
it's very specific.

00:20:33.610 --> 00:20:38.340
So first of all, your app may
not run with those policies.

00:20:38.340 --> 00:20:40.870
So isolation-wise
we have another one

00:20:40.870 --> 00:20:44.700
called making a VN as isolation
boundary, which is true.

00:20:44.700 --> 00:20:46.450
But then the downside
there is that, well,

00:20:46.450 --> 00:20:49.840
you have to launch VN, and
then you have to run a kernel--

00:20:49.840 --> 00:20:52.270
large or small, it's
still a kernel--

00:20:52.270 --> 00:20:55.490
for just one container
or one process.

00:20:55.490 --> 00:20:58.220
It's not necessarily
effective or elastic

00:20:58.220 --> 00:20:59.330
as a regular process.

00:20:59.330 --> 00:21:01.970
So OK, so we talk
about various things.

00:21:01.970 --> 00:21:03.800
There are some pros and cons.

00:21:03.800 --> 00:21:07.260
So what do you think?

00:21:07.260 --> 00:21:09.860
Do we have all the
right tools for us?

00:21:09.860 --> 00:21:10.880
What do you want?

00:21:10.880 --> 00:21:12.050
IAN LEWIS: Right.

00:21:12.050 --> 00:21:17.390
So at Google, we looked at a
number of different solutions.

00:21:17.390 --> 00:21:21.440
We talked about, we looked
at things like Seccomp,

00:21:21.440 --> 00:21:24.500
we look at things like
SELinux and AppArmor,

00:21:24.500 --> 00:21:27.170
we looked at things
like VM Solutions,

00:21:27.170 --> 00:21:29.970
and there's trade-offs between
all of these different things.

00:21:29.970 --> 00:21:32.834
And so the thing
that we saw that we

00:21:32.834 --> 00:21:34.250
would have to make
trade-offs with

00:21:34.250 --> 00:21:38.970
is that we wanted our
containers to be secure

00:21:38.970 --> 00:21:42.230
so that attackers that have
gotten control of the container

00:21:42.230 --> 00:21:44.390
can't break out
of the container.

00:21:44.390 --> 00:21:47.120
We wanted something that was,
like, less configuration,

00:21:47.120 --> 00:21:49.640
or ideally zero
configuration, something

00:21:49.640 --> 00:21:52.340
that was kind of like
a self-driving car,

00:21:52.340 --> 00:21:55.070
here, where it's kind of
turnkey solution-- something we

00:21:55.070 --> 00:21:56.660
can just get in and go.

00:21:56.660 --> 00:21:59.780
We don't have to
configure per application,

00:21:59.780 --> 00:22:03.020
you know, filters, or like per
application rights, some sort

00:22:03.020 --> 00:22:06.590
of SE Linux or AppArmor policy.

00:22:06.590 --> 00:22:10.310
We want to just be able to run
our applications unmodified.

00:22:10.310 --> 00:22:12.410
Another was that
we wanted to have

00:22:12.410 --> 00:22:14.300
something that was lightweight.

00:22:14.300 --> 00:22:17.240
We ran a lot of
containers, and so we

00:22:17.240 --> 00:22:19.670
wanted something that
gave a similar experience

00:22:19.670 --> 00:22:20.870
to what we were used to.

00:22:20.870 --> 00:22:23.210
So we want something
that's lightweight, doesn't

00:22:23.210 --> 00:22:26.920
use a lot of resources,
and gives us lower load

00:22:26.920 --> 00:22:29.485
memory and CPU overhead.

00:22:29.485 --> 00:22:30.860
And so we looked
at these, and we

00:22:30.860 --> 00:22:33.440
saw that we had to make
a lot of trade-offs,

00:22:33.440 --> 00:22:38.210
but we thought what if we didn't
have to make these trade-offs.

00:22:38.210 --> 00:22:43.820
What if we could get everything
we wanted in a system?

00:22:43.820 --> 00:22:45.830
We could maybe make
different trade-offs

00:22:45.830 --> 00:22:49.320
and then get all of the
things that we wanted here.

00:22:49.320 --> 00:22:52.050
And so we came up with
something called gVisor.

00:22:52.050 --> 00:22:56.190
So gVisor is a sandbox
for containers,

00:22:56.190 --> 00:23:01.650
and it implements a
independent user space kernel.

00:23:01.650 --> 00:23:06.270
And so this runs in user space,
separate from the Linux kernel,

00:23:06.270 --> 00:23:10.600
so it gives you an extra
level of isolation.

00:23:10.600 --> 00:23:12.840
And so as we were doing
this, we're like, you know,

00:23:12.840 --> 00:23:19.280
the Linux system call interface
is like this wide attack

00:23:19.280 --> 00:23:21.070
surface.

00:23:21.070 --> 00:23:23.140
But you can think of
it kind of like an API.

00:23:23.140 --> 00:23:26.830
The Linux kernel
provides you an API

00:23:26.830 --> 00:23:30.760
that your applications then use
to execute themselves and get

00:23:30.760 --> 00:23:33.010
resources, so what
if we just made

00:23:33.010 --> 00:23:35.470
a different implementation
of that API,

00:23:35.470 --> 00:23:37.060
and we ran that in userspace?

00:23:37.060 --> 00:23:40.060
And that's how we came up
with the idea for gVisor.

00:23:40.060 --> 00:23:43.450
And so it's a user
space kernel that

00:23:43.450 --> 00:23:45.130
implements these system calls.

00:23:45.130 --> 00:23:48.820
So it's similar to something
like user space Linux,

00:23:48.820 --> 00:23:51.970
except we have our own kernel
rather than trying to run Linux

00:23:51.970 --> 00:23:55.850
itself in user space.

00:23:55.850 --> 00:23:59.450
And so this gives us the kind
of high security that we want.

00:23:59.450 --> 00:24:03.980
It's a user space kernel that
implements these syscalls,

00:24:03.980 --> 00:24:05.870
and it's written in Go.

00:24:05.870 --> 00:24:09.170
And the reason why
we did it in Go

00:24:09.170 --> 00:24:11.180
is because we got
a good trade-offs

00:24:11.180 --> 00:24:18.020
when using Go between the memory
and type safety of the language

00:24:18.020 --> 00:24:21.740
runtime, as well as the speed.

00:24:21.740 --> 00:24:24.860
And we also get this kind
of Linux user space kernel,

00:24:24.860 --> 00:24:30.050
so that even if the attacker
gets control of the container

00:24:30.050 --> 00:24:35.960
application, then they find
some sort of bug in the gVisor,

00:24:35.960 --> 00:24:37.580
even if they do that
they still don't

00:24:37.580 --> 00:24:38.780
have control of the system.

00:24:38.780 --> 00:24:40.610
They're still stuck
in user space,

00:24:40.610 --> 00:24:44.060
so they can't just execute
everything as the kernel

00:24:44.060 --> 00:24:45.740
and get full access
to the whole host.

00:24:48.790 --> 00:24:51.020
gVisor is also zero config.

00:24:51.020 --> 00:24:53.570
We can run unmodified
Linux applications

00:24:53.570 --> 00:24:58.030
because we implement
the Linux syscalls.

00:24:58.030 --> 00:25:02.240
Applications can-- we can
just run them in the sandbox,

00:25:02.240 --> 00:25:07.490
and have them use that kernel.

00:25:07.490 --> 00:25:12.470
And so we don't need to write
any filters or profiles--

00:25:12.470 --> 00:25:15.680
like security profiles,
things like that-- in order

00:25:15.680 --> 00:25:20.690
to get the lease privilege
for our applications.

00:25:20.690 --> 00:25:22.190
Typically for those
type of filters,

00:25:22.190 --> 00:25:24.231
you would need to know
what the application needs

00:25:24.231 --> 00:25:26.550
to use, what
capabilities it needs,

00:25:26.550 --> 00:25:31.130
what syscalls it's using,
and what sort of resources

00:25:31.130 --> 00:25:32.660
it needs from the
operating system

00:25:32.660 --> 00:25:36.470
in order to actually
configure your lease

00:25:36.470 --> 00:25:38.870
privilege for the application.

00:25:38.870 --> 00:25:42.080
But with gVisor, we can just run
the application in the sandbox

00:25:42.080 --> 00:25:44.821
and we don't need to do
these kind of configurations.

00:25:47.770 --> 00:25:49.910
It's also very light and quick.

00:25:49.910 --> 00:25:52.330
So it's very similar to
the kind of experience

00:25:52.330 --> 00:25:55.750
that we got when running
normal containers.

00:25:55.750 --> 00:25:59.890
So we have basically a
15-megabyte or so memory

00:25:59.890 --> 00:26:00.500
footprint.

00:26:00.500 --> 00:26:03.130
This can kind of change
depending on the application

00:26:03.130 --> 00:26:07.630
and how it actually uses
the gVisor userspace kernel.

00:26:07.630 --> 00:26:11.830
But in general you get something
around a 15-megabyte memory

00:26:11.830 --> 00:26:13.550
footprint.

00:26:13.550 --> 00:26:15.820
So you get just that
amount of overhead,

00:26:15.820 --> 00:26:17.770
and also you can
start up applications

00:26:17.770 --> 00:26:19.730
in around 150 milliseconds.

00:26:19.730 --> 00:26:23.200
So this is very similar to the
way that the containers run.

00:26:23.200 --> 00:26:27.310
We don't have to have
a lot of startup time

00:26:27.310 --> 00:26:31.510
or a lot of overhead
associated with our sandbox.

00:26:31.510 --> 00:26:34.390
So this really fit well
with our kind of model,

00:26:34.390 --> 00:26:38.590
and fit well with our container
orchestration within Google.

00:26:42.350 --> 00:26:47.170
And so we use gVisor for a
number of things within Google,

00:26:47.170 --> 00:26:48.820
but one of the more
obvious things--

00:26:48.820 --> 00:26:51.400
like Yoshi mentioned--
and this is a tweet

00:26:51.400 --> 00:26:54.460
from our director, Oren.

00:26:54.460 --> 00:26:57.190
We use it within cloud
platform particularly

00:26:57.190 --> 00:27:00.910
for things where users need
to upload application code,

00:27:00.910 --> 00:27:02.900
and we run it on their behalf.

00:27:02.900 --> 00:27:05.350
So we don't want to have
a situation where somebody

00:27:05.350 --> 00:27:07.840
can upload code and then get
access to somebody else's

00:27:07.840 --> 00:27:10.390
application, or break
out of a container

00:27:10.390 --> 00:27:15.520
and then read some
other user's data.

00:27:15.520 --> 00:27:18.380
That would be a pretty
bad security breach.

00:27:18.380 --> 00:27:20.500
So what we want is
to provide a sandbox

00:27:20.500 --> 00:27:22.870
to run these applications.

00:27:22.870 --> 00:27:25.900
And so for things like App
Engine, Cloud Functions,

00:27:25.900 --> 00:27:27.940
and Cloud ML.

00:27:27.940 --> 00:27:30.580
This is a perfect
fit for us in order

00:27:30.580 --> 00:27:32.870
to run applications within
a sandbox like this.

00:27:36.000 --> 00:27:40.320
And then we decided that this
was a particularly interesting

00:27:40.320 --> 00:27:41.910
technology, and
this is something

00:27:41.910 --> 00:27:43.980
that, now that
containers are becoming

00:27:43.980 --> 00:27:47.910
more ubiquitous
and more popular,

00:27:47.910 --> 00:27:50.779
and things like orchestration
systems like Kubernetes

00:27:50.779 --> 00:27:53.070
are starting to become popular,
and people are starting

00:27:53.070 --> 00:27:57.812
to build passes, and all kinds
of different systems on top,

00:27:57.812 --> 00:27:59.520
we thought that gVisor
would be something

00:27:59.520 --> 00:28:01.770
that users outside
of Google could

00:28:01.770 --> 00:28:04.830
take advantage of
in order to provide

00:28:04.830 --> 00:28:08.540
these type of solutions.

00:28:08.540 --> 00:28:11.570
And so we open sourced
it, and now it's

00:28:11.570 --> 00:28:15.470
on GitHub at
GitHub/Google/gVisor.

00:28:15.470 --> 00:28:19.340
We open sourced it in
May this year at KubeCon,

00:28:19.340 --> 00:28:22.070
and the response was enormous.

00:28:22.070 --> 00:28:25.550
Like, it was quite a lot
more than we expected.

00:28:25.550 --> 00:28:30.380
Within 10 days we had
5,000 GitHub stars,

00:28:30.380 --> 00:28:35.490
and we have over 6,400 today.

00:28:35.490 --> 00:28:40.910
So thanks to all of you who
have started us on GitHub

00:28:40.910 --> 00:28:44.570
and who are kind of
following a project.

00:28:44.570 --> 00:28:46.860
But the response
has been enormous,

00:28:46.860 --> 00:28:49.040
and we're really
kind of interested

00:28:49.040 --> 00:28:57.860
in how we can take this
further and integrate this

00:28:57.860 --> 00:29:01.940
with Kubernetes and other
orchestration systems.

00:29:01.940 --> 00:29:04.220
But before we do that,
let's take a look back

00:29:04.220 --> 00:29:08.690
at the actual architecture,
and dive a little bit deeper

00:29:08.690 --> 00:29:12.710
into the architecture of gVisor.

00:29:12.710 --> 00:29:17.240
So gVisor is a system
for running a sandbox.

00:29:17.240 --> 00:29:19.640
And so we have our
user space kernel,

00:29:19.640 --> 00:29:23.450
but we wanted to make something
that made it easy for folks

00:29:23.450 --> 00:29:25.700
to run in containers
in our sandbox.

00:29:25.700 --> 00:29:31.740
And so we provide an
OCI-compatible interface.

00:29:31.740 --> 00:29:33.410
So if you're a
Docker user you may

00:29:33.410 --> 00:29:41.480
be familiar with runc, which is
the OCI runtime for containers.

00:29:41.480 --> 00:29:44.930
And gVisor provides a
command called runsc

00:29:44.930 --> 00:29:49.700
that is essentially a
drop-in replacement for runc,

00:29:49.700 --> 00:29:52.940
and provides this
OCI implementation.

00:29:52.940 --> 00:29:56.810
So we can run OCI bundles
for containers in a sandbox

00:29:56.810 --> 00:29:59.640
very easily.

00:29:59.640 --> 00:30:03.210
And so once you
start up a container,

00:30:03.210 --> 00:30:08.460
that container's application
is loaded into memory

00:30:08.460 --> 00:30:15.750
along with the sentry, which
is our kernel in userspace.

00:30:15.750 --> 00:30:20.970
And the application is loaded
into the sentry's user memory.

00:30:20.970 --> 00:30:23.100
And then the
application is executed.

00:30:23.100 --> 00:30:25.440
But as we are
executing it the sentry

00:30:25.440 --> 00:30:27.220
has two different
types of modes.

00:30:27.220 --> 00:30:30.720
One is a KVM mode, and
one is a ptrace mode.

00:30:30.720 --> 00:30:34.590
And what these do is these
intercept the syscalls

00:30:34.590 --> 00:30:37.740
that the application makes
and then handles them

00:30:37.740 --> 00:30:40.920
inside the sentry-- so inside
of the userspace kernel.

00:30:40.920 --> 00:30:46.140
So we don't actually
go into the kernel

00:30:46.140 --> 00:30:50.190
in order to handle a lot of
the syscalls that we're making.

00:30:50.190 --> 00:30:51.810
The sentry does
actually have to make

00:30:51.810 --> 00:30:57.870
a few syscalls to the
Linux kernel itself,

00:30:57.870 --> 00:31:00.960
but that surface area
is highly reduced

00:31:00.960 --> 00:31:02.950
compared to a normal container.

00:31:02.950 --> 00:31:07.860
So Yoshi mentioned that
we had 319 total syscalls

00:31:07.860 --> 00:31:12.930
for the Linux syscall API, and
the sentry narrows that down

00:31:12.930 --> 00:31:15.720
to a surface area of
something along the lines

00:31:15.720 --> 00:31:17.750
of between 10 and 20.

00:31:17.750 --> 00:31:21.110
So something like that.

00:31:21.110 --> 00:31:23.090
And because we know
what the sentry needs

00:31:23.090 --> 00:31:27.680
in terms of its own syscalls,
we can then put a satcom profile

00:31:27.680 --> 00:31:32.510
on that and further lock down
so that only those syscalls are

00:31:32.510 --> 00:31:36.480
the ones that can actually
be run by the sentry.

00:31:36.480 --> 00:31:40.410
And so even if somebody
is able to take control

00:31:40.410 --> 00:31:43.050
of an application and then
they find some sort of bug

00:31:43.050 --> 00:31:45.630
in the sentry-- some sort of
buffer overrun or something

00:31:45.630 --> 00:31:49.440
like that-- that they're able
to execute code as the sentry,

00:31:49.440 --> 00:31:52.630
they're still locked into
this userspace sandbox.

00:31:52.630 --> 00:31:55.950
They can only run
a few syscalls.

00:31:55.950 --> 00:31:59.730
And so you're not actually
able to do anything

00:31:59.730 --> 00:32:02.810
very much at that point.

00:32:02.810 --> 00:32:06.470
The sandbox actually can't
even write to the file system.

00:32:06.470 --> 00:32:09.110
When actually locations need
to write to the file system,

00:32:09.110 --> 00:32:13.340
the sentry uses a
separate process

00:32:13.340 --> 00:32:17.330
that we start up for the
container called a gofer.

00:32:17.330 --> 00:32:19.370
And we use the 9P
protocol in order

00:32:19.370 --> 00:32:25.190
to send our to do file I/O
and things like network I/O.

00:32:25.190 --> 00:32:27.710
So that type of I/O
is not something

00:32:27.710 --> 00:32:31.760
that you can actually do
just unlimited, even if you

00:32:31.760 --> 00:32:34.490
can execute code as the sentry.

00:32:34.490 --> 00:32:40.310
So this gives us a quite a
good sandbox for applications

00:32:40.310 --> 00:32:41.960
to run for untrusted code.

00:32:41.960 --> 00:32:45.070
It gives us a very good
isolation from the kernel.

00:32:49.830 --> 00:32:55.590
But as we said, everything that
you do is trade-offs, right?

00:32:55.590 --> 00:32:57.540
I said that earlier
that we didn't

00:32:57.540 --> 00:33:00.540
want to make the
trade-offs between security

00:33:00.540 --> 00:33:05.610
and zero config, and having
lightweight containers,

00:33:05.610 --> 00:33:07.980
but that means that we
have to make trade-offs

00:33:07.980 --> 00:33:10.170
in some other places.

00:33:10.170 --> 00:33:12.600
And so there are
some things that we

00:33:12.600 --> 00:33:16.110
have to add as caveats to
running things in gVisor.

00:33:18.340 --> 00:33:20.340
Let's walk through some
of the applications that

00:33:20.340 --> 00:33:26.660
might be impacted by
running in a gVisor sandbox.

00:33:26.660 --> 00:33:29.610
The first of those is
syscall heavy workloads.

00:33:29.610 --> 00:33:33.800
So because gVisor is
implementing these syscalls

00:33:33.800 --> 00:33:40.280
in user space, it's a kernel
that's using the go runtime,

00:33:40.280 --> 00:33:44.940
and just by the fact of
being running user space,

00:33:44.940 --> 00:33:46.820
there is a slight
difference in things,

00:33:46.820 --> 00:33:50.590
like running kernel
code is running

00:33:50.590 --> 00:33:53.220
is a higher priority on on
the CPU, and things like that.

00:33:53.220 --> 00:33:55.650
So you will get some
performance impact.

00:33:55.650 --> 00:33:58.820
So applications
that might run lots

00:33:58.820 --> 00:34:00.710
of syscalls in a
tight loop, those

00:34:00.710 --> 00:34:02.300
are the types of
applications that

00:34:02.300 --> 00:34:06.110
are going to be impacted
performance-wise

00:34:06.110 --> 00:34:09.030
by running in gVisor.

00:34:09.030 --> 00:34:12.699
There's also the idea of syscall
coverage and compatibility.

00:34:12.699 --> 00:34:16.550
So gVisor itself implements
many of the Linux syscalls,

00:34:16.550 --> 00:34:19.710
but it doesn't implement every
single last one of them--

00:34:19.710 --> 00:34:21.550
at least not currently.

00:34:21.550 --> 00:34:28.500
And so there's a total of 319,
but we implement around 211,

00:34:28.500 --> 00:34:31.542
and those are some of the
more highly-used ones,

00:34:31.542 --> 00:34:33.000
but it also means
that applications

00:34:33.000 --> 00:34:35.580
that use those syscalls
that we don't implement

00:34:35.580 --> 00:34:37.889
will have some
compatibility issues.

00:34:42.699 --> 00:34:45.790
The last thing that I want
to highlight as a caveat

00:34:45.790 --> 00:34:47.710
is direct access to hardware.

00:34:47.710 --> 00:34:54.080
So applications that need
direct access to hardware

00:34:54.080 --> 00:34:57.330
are potentially not a great
fit for gVisor, and partly

00:34:57.330 --> 00:34:59.250
because just of the
nature of the fact

00:34:59.250 --> 00:35:02.130
that we are running
in a sandbox.

00:35:02.130 --> 00:35:03.720
Providing direct
access to hardware

00:35:03.720 --> 00:35:05.610
from a security
standpoint is very risky

00:35:05.610 --> 00:35:08.630
because you have direct
access to the hardware.

00:35:08.630 --> 00:35:09.960
You can do whatever you want.

00:35:09.960 --> 00:35:13.500
So if the application is
compromised the entire host

00:35:13.500 --> 00:35:14.580
is generally compromised.

00:35:14.580 --> 00:35:17.250
And so gVisor doesn't
have the concept

00:35:17.250 --> 00:35:21.150
that a normal Linux kernel does
of device drivers and things

00:35:21.150 --> 00:35:23.610
like that, and so direct
access to hardware

00:35:23.610 --> 00:35:26.550
like GPUs or other
specialty hardware

00:35:26.550 --> 00:35:32.320
may not be a good fit for
gVisor, at least not right now.

00:35:32.320 --> 00:35:34.649
Great.

00:35:34.649 --> 00:35:35.690
So that's out of the way.

00:35:35.690 --> 00:35:41.060
Let's actually take a look at a
couple of the vulnerabilities.

00:35:41.060 --> 00:35:45.800
The first one is a Dirty COW,
and the second one is Meltdown.

00:35:45.800 --> 00:35:47.450
And we'll just
kind of take a look

00:35:47.450 --> 00:35:50.300
at how gVisor might
mitigate a couple

00:35:50.300 --> 00:35:55.130
of these vulnerabilities,
and explain what they do

00:35:55.130 --> 00:35:58.050
and then how gVisor
kind of mitigates them.

00:35:58.050 --> 00:36:00.200
Thank you.

00:36:00.200 --> 00:36:01.420
All right.

00:36:01.420 --> 00:36:03.580
So let's switch over
to the demo machine.

00:36:11.680 --> 00:36:13.150
YOSHI TAMURA: Can we switch?

00:36:13.150 --> 00:36:16.770
IAN LEWIS: Yeah, can we switch
over to the demo machine here?

00:36:16.770 --> 00:36:18.090
YOSHI TAMURA: Thank you.

00:36:18.090 --> 00:36:19.810
IAN LEWIS: All right, great.

00:36:19.810 --> 00:36:21.920
Let's make this a
little bit bigger.

00:36:21.920 --> 00:36:25.570
So what I'm going
to do here is, I'm

00:36:25.570 --> 00:36:30.570
going to run an application
that makes use of the Dirty COW

00:36:30.570 --> 00:36:34.180
exploit, and run this in
just a normal container

00:36:34.180 --> 00:36:38.000
to show you what happens
when you do that.

00:36:38.000 --> 00:36:41.650
So what happens here
is what I have here is

00:36:41.650 --> 00:36:44.650
I have an OCI bundle.

00:36:44.650 --> 00:36:46.220
So this is just a
regular container.

00:36:46.220 --> 00:36:48.345
So you can think of this
as just a container that's

00:36:48.345 --> 00:36:50.290
been unpacked, or a
container image that's

00:36:50.290 --> 00:36:51.790
been unpacked to
the file system,

00:36:51.790 --> 00:36:55.630
and we're going to
run this using runc.

00:36:55.630 --> 00:37:03.019
So if we just do like an
n20 here with a config,

00:37:03.019 --> 00:37:05.060
we can see we're going to
run an application here

00:37:05.060 --> 00:37:06.680
inside the container,
we're going

00:37:06.680 --> 00:37:08.810
to write some data
to the exec password.

00:37:08.810 --> 00:37:11.720
We're running this as
a user, not as root.

00:37:11.720 --> 00:37:15.039
So normally the user wouldn't
be able to write to this file.

00:37:15.039 --> 00:37:16.580
What we're going to
do is we're going

00:37:16.580 --> 00:37:19.010
to write this little
string here, spam,

00:37:19.010 --> 00:37:22.677
into the exact password
file, which is not something

00:37:22.677 --> 00:37:24.260
that you would
normally be able to do,

00:37:24.260 --> 00:37:26.520
but because of Dirty COW
exploiting Dirty COW,

00:37:26.520 --> 00:37:28.920
you're able to do that.

00:37:28.920 --> 00:37:31.700
So when we run it inside
a normal container--

00:37:31.700 --> 00:37:35.854
we're going to do sudo runc run.

00:37:35.854 --> 00:37:37.020
We've to give it to me here.

00:37:37.020 --> 00:37:38.020
We'll just say dirtycow.

00:37:41.800 --> 00:37:44.270
So when we run this
application, what this is doing

00:37:44.270 --> 00:37:48.170
is it's taking advantage of
a race condition in the Linux

00:37:48.170 --> 00:37:49.040
kernel.

00:37:49.040 --> 00:37:51.530
What this is doing is
doing a couple of syscalls

00:37:51.530 --> 00:37:54.410
in two different threads
at the same time in kind

00:37:54.410 --> 00:37:59.640
of a tight loop, and when
it gets to the kernel

00:37:59.640 --> 00:38:04.590
state in just the right place,
it's able to write to a file

00:38:04.590 --> 00:38:09.290
regardless of what permissions
are associated with that file.

00:38:09.290 --> 00:38:14.750
And so what we're
doing here, this

00:38:14.750 --> 00:38:17.960
uses two different syscalls.

00:38:17.960 --> 00:38:20.740
One of them is the
madvise syscall

00:38:20.740 --> 00:38:22.480
and one is a ptrace syscall.

00:38:22.480 --> 00:38:24.885
But the madvise
syscall is one syscall

00:38:24.885 --> 00:38:27.730
that is highly used in
normal applications.

00:38:27.730 --> 00:38:30.250
So this is something that's
very hard to actually filter out

00:38:30.250 --> 00:38:31.060
of an application.

00:38:31.060 --> 00:38:33.700
So like, you know, you
can't necessarily just

00:38:33.700 --> 00:38:38.260
put on a setcom policy that you
can't run madvise or something

00:38:38.260 --> 00:38:40.309
like that, because
madvise is used

00:38:40.309 --> 00:38:42.100
in pretty much every
application out there.

00:38:42.100 --> 00:38:45.970
So even if you're able to know
what your application uses

00:38:45.970 --> 00:38:49.510
for things like syscalls,
you're not always

00:38:49.510 --> 00:38:52.690
able to just filter
out those calls.

00:38:52.690 --> 00:38:55.750
Some of them you actually
need to be using.

00:38:55.750 --> 00:38:57.940
OK, so here the
application's finished.

00:38:57.940 --> 00:39:02.440
It's run through our
tight loop and has

00:39:02.440 --> 00:39:05.260
been able to take
advantage of Dirty COW.

00:39:05.260 --> 00:39:08.710
And so what we'll do here is
we'll take a look at the--

00:39:08.710 --> 00:39:13.626
actually we need to look at
the root file system here.

00:39:13.626 --> 00:39:18.620
And we'll look at the password
file, and scroll up here.

00:39:18.620 --> 00:39:21.020
So we're actually able
see here that we've

00:39:21.020 --> 00:39:23.460
written to the beginning
of this file, here.

00:39:23.460 --> 00:39:25.820
So you can imagine
that we could overwrite

00:39:25.820 --> 00:39:29.270
just lines in this file,
add a new root password,

00:39:29.270 --> 00:39:32.240
and just get privilege
escalation that way.

00:39:32.240 --> 00:39:34.730
So it's very easy to
get privilege escalation

00:39:34.730 --> 00:39:37.730
when you can just write to
any file on the file system.

00:39:37.730 --> 00:39:41.950
So this is something that's
really, really bad for us.

00:39:41.950 --> 00:39:47.290
So let's switch over and take
a look at running it in gVisor.

00:39:47.290 --> 00:39:53.420
So when we do this, we have
the exact same OCI bundle.

00:39:53.420 --> 00:39:54.700
We're going to do a--

00:39:59.880 --> 00:40:02.540
show you here, just the
exact same configuration.

00:40:02.540 --> 00:40:04.150
We're running as the same user.

00:40:04.150 --> 00:40:06.580
We're using the
same application.

00:40:06.580 --> 00:40:11.320
We're going to write to the
same file with the same content,

00:40:11.320 --> 00:40:13.450
but this time we're going
to use runsc gVisor.

00:40:13.450 --> 00:40:16.690
So we're going to
do sudo, runsc.

00:40:16.690 --> 00:40:19.020
I'm going to add a couple
of flags in here just

00:40:19.020 --> 00:40:20.770
to show some debug
information to show you

00:40:20.770 --> 00:40:22.690
what's going on, because
otherwise it would just kind

00:40:22.690 --> 00:40:23.523
of sit here and run.

00:40:31.410 --> 00:40:33.819
All right, so when
we run it in gVisor,

00:40:33.819 --> 00:40:35.610
we're able to actually
run the application.

00:40:35.610 --> 00:40:38.010
And the application executes
exactly the same way

00:40:38.010 --> 00:40:42.870
as it would inside of a normal
container, except for what it's

00:40:42.870 --> 00:40:45.420
doing here is it's running
in two different loops

00:40:45.420 --> 00:40:49.140
or in two different threads
and executing madvise

00:40:49.140 --> 00:40:50.590
and ptrace over and over again.

00:40:50.590 --> 00:40:53.160
And so what this is doing
is just spitting out

00:40:53.160 --> 00:40:56.472
logs every time the madvise
and ptrace are called.

00:40:56.472 --> 00:40:58.680
But you can see here that
the application is actually

00:40:58.680 --> 00:41:02.220
running, but in the
end we won't actually

00:41:02.220 --> 00:41:05.760
be able to write to the file.

00:41:05.760 --> 00:41:07.280
So we go in here, actually.

00:41:07.280 --> 00:41:13.020
I should open up another SSH.

00:41:13.020 --> 00:41:15.780
And we'll just look at
the file and then verify

00:41:15.780 --> 00:41:18.349
that the file has not
actually been written to.

00:41:37.830 --> 00:41:39.630
Let's do this, all
right, and then we

00:41:39.630 --> 00:41:43.880
say root file system,
exec password.

00:41:48.369 --> 00:41:49.160
So we look up here.

00:41:49.160 --> 00:41:50.618
We can see that we
haven't actually

00:41:50.618 --> 00:41:52.460
been able to write to this.

00:41:52.460 --> 00:41:55.130
And by nature, this particular
attack-- the attacker

00:41:55.130 --> 00:41:56.810
will just continue
to run and try

00:41:56.810 --> 00:42:00.200
to take advantage of
the kernel, and as long

00:42:00.200 --> 00:42:05.810
as it's not able to, it's just
not able to write to the file.

00:42:05.810 --> 00:42:07.550
But it's also kind
of interesting

00:42:07.550 --> 00:42:10.220
to note that this is exactly
the type of application that

00:42:10.220 --> 00:42:13.520
can have a performance
impact in gVisor,

00:42:13.520 --> 00:42:16.430
because it's running
in a tight loop

00:42:16.430 --> 00:42:18.320
and just executing syscalls.

00:42:18.320 --> 00:42:21.260
This is actually a worst
case scenario for that.

00:42:21.260 --> 00:42:24.860
But normal applications aren't
doing this type of thing.

00:42:30.480 --> 00:42:35.090
So we're able to see that we
can mitigate vulnerabilities

00:42:35.090 --> 00:42:38.060
with, like, Dirty COW, and
that's a kind of a pure Linux

00:42:38.060 --> 00:42:40.490
kernel vulnerability.

00:42:40.490 --> 00:42:43.130
But what about for other
kinds of vulnerabilities?

00:42:43.130 --> 00:42:45.230
So Meltdown is
another vulnerability

00:42:45.230 --> 00:42:48.270
that's very popular.

00:42:48.270 --> 00:42:51.560
And meltdown is a combination
of hardware vulnerability

00:42:51.560 --> 00:42:53.660
as well as software
vulnerability,

00:42:53.660 --> 00:42:59.180
so this is something that,
because gVisor is a software

00:42:59.180 --> 00:43:03.110
sandbox we can't 100%
mitigate vulnerabilities,

00:43:03.110 --> 00:43:04.860
but in some sense
on the software side

00:43:04.860 --> 00:43:06.960
we can mitigate it a little bit.

00:43:06.960 --> 00:43:13.940
So for instance, we are
going to run an application

00:43:13.940 --> 00:43:15.830
and use Meltdown.

00:43:15.830 --> 00:43:22.900
And what Meltdown
does is, it takes

00:43:22.900 --> 00:43:26.390
advantage of a hardware bug in
kind of conditional execution.

00:43:30.740 --> 00:43:33.610
But the most easy way to
take advantage of that

00:43:33.610 --> 00:43:39.100
is in a situation where in
the past, when applications

00:43:39.100 --> 00:43:43.190
were run they would map kernel
memory into the process itself.

00:43:43.190 --> 00:43:48.190
And so if you do that and
you have this hardware bug,

00:43:48.190 --> 00:43:52.480
you're very easily able
to have the application be

00:43:52.480 --> 00:43:56.650
able to read that kernel
memory in any place--

00:43:56.650 --> 00:43:59.480
and not just places that
you're able to read,

00:43:59.480 --> 00:44:02.540
but ones that you
shouldn't be able to read.

00:44:02.540 --> 00:44:11.200
So here what we're
going to do is

00:44:11.200 --> 00:44:14.451
we're going to get the memory
address of the Linux prok

00:44:14.451 --> 00:44:14.950
banner.

00:44:14.950 --> 00:44:18.140
So this is something that's
part of kernel memory.

00:44:18.140 --> 00:44:22.116
And so when an application
is run this kernel memory is

00:44:22.116 --> 00:44:24.490
mapped in, and then what we're
going to do is we're going

00:44:24.490 --> 00:44:27.115
to tell our application to read
this particular piece of kernel

00:44:27.115 --> 00:44:29.200
memory-- this address
from kernel memory--

00:44:29.200 --> 00:44:31.090
that it normally
shouldn't be able to read.

00:44:33.930 --> 00:44:34.930
So we just look at this.

00:44:34.930 --> 00:44:45.460
It's actually-- this is
just an address in memory

00:44:45.460 --> 00:44:47.480
that we want to try to read.

00:44:47.480 --> 00:44:50.031
So if we run this in a
normal Docker container--

00:45:04.770 --> 00:45:08.270
So we're going to run this
in a normal Docker container

00:45:08.270 --> 00:45:11.220
with runc, and we're
going to give it

00:45:11.220 --> 00:45:14.300
the address that we
have here and have

00:45:14.300 --> 00:45:15.720
it read out that memory.

00:45:18.450 --> 00:45:20.940
So when we do that,
we're able to see

00:45:20.940 --> 00:45:23.220
that the application
is vulnerable

00:45:23.220 --> 00:45:25.824
and that we're able to
read out of the memory.

00:45:25.824 --> 00:45:27.240
And the Linux prok
banner just has

00:45:27.240 --> 00:45:29.280
some information about
the kernel version,

00:45:29.280 --> 00:45:31.570
and some other information.

00:45:31.570 --> 00:45:34.380
So that's not particularly bad.

00:45:34.380 --> 00:45:37.560
But reading other
parts of kernel memory

00:45:37.560 --> 00:45:40.860
could potentially give
you lots and lots-- like,

00:45:40.860 --> 00:45:43.230
allow you to do lots of
different types of privilege

00:45:43.230 --> 00:45:45.330
escalation.

00:45:45.330 --> 00:45:48.510
So when we bring this
using the same way,

00:45:48.510 --> 00:45:51.660
but we run it in
gVisor, gVisor actually

00:45:51.660 --> 00:45:57.270
doesn't map in
this kernel memory,

00:45:57.270 --> 00:46:03.276
and so we're able to
mitigate this slightly.

00:46:03.276 --> 00:46:04.733
I didn't run this with sudo.

00:46:08.177 --> 00:46:08.677
Oh no.

00:46:25.932 --> 00:46:27.904
AUDIENCE: Put it
before the meltdown.

00:46:27.904 --> 00:46:30.080
IAN LEWIS: Yes, you are right.

00:46:48.860 --> 00:46:52.922
So we run this
with gVisor, we're

00:46:52.922 --> 00:46:54.380
able to see that
it's not runnable.

00:46:54.380 --> 00:46:57.710
And because the kernel memory
is not mapped into the process,

00:46:57.710 --> 00:47:00.440
we're not able to actually
read that memory out

00:47:00.440 --> 00:47:02.780
from Linux prok banner.

00:47:02.780 --> 00:47:06.950
So in that sense, some things
can be partially mitigated.

00:47:06.950 --> 00:47:09.770
There are other theoretical ways
that you could take advantage

00:47:09.770 --> 00:47:12.440
of Meltdown in
gVisor, but those are

00:47:12.440 --> 00:47:19.920
things that we think are a
little bit harder to actually

00:47:19.920 --> 00:47:24.275
get working, because gVisor
has a hardend sandbox.

00:47:27.040 --> 00:47:29.762
All right, great.

00:47:29.762 --> 00:47:30.970
YOSHI TAMURA: Thank you, Ian.

00:47:30.970 --> 00:47:33.317
Then back to the slides please.

00:47:33.317 --> 00:47:34.900
These is our probably
first time doing

00:47:34.900 --> 00:47:36.030
the demonstration on stage.

00:47:36.030 --> 00:47:38.430
So it was great to
actually see it in action.

00:47:38.430 --> 00:47:39.120
[APPLAUSE]

00:47:39.120 --> 00:47:40.080
Thank you.

00:47:40.080 --> 00:47:41.020
Appreciate it.

00:47:45.720 --> 00:47:49.380
So gVisor and Kubernetes--

00:47:49.380 --> 00:47:54.960
both were invented at
Google, and we democratized

00:47:54.960 --> 00:47:57.120
that with open source--

00:47:57.120 --> 00:47:58.570
both Kubernetes and gVisor.

00:47:58.570 --> 00:48:00.060
And by the way,
gVisor, if you like

00:48:00.060 --> 00:48:02.226
it there are a couple of
things you could do for us.

00:48:02.226 --> 00:48:04.770
You could go to GitHub,
press star button.

00:48:04.770 --> 00:48:05.520
You could Fork it.

00:48:05.520 --> 00:48:06.620
We have our 300 Forks.

00:48:06.620 --> 00:48:08.697
So if you have
interesting projects,

00:48:08.697 --> 00:48:09.780
absolutely please Fork it.

00:48:09.780 --> 00:48:12.080
We would love to hear
what it could do.

00:48:12.080 --> 00:48:13.680
And also, perhaps
most importantly--

00:48:13.680 --> 00:48:15.070
like Ian mentioned--

00:48:15.070 --> 00:48:18.990
gVisor took a different
kind of trade-off,

00:48:18.990 --> 00:48:21.280
which was Cisco compatibility.

00:48:21.280 --> 00:48:24.750
If you can try running your
application and it didn't run,

00:48:24.750 --> 00:48:27.960
file a bug, let us know, and
let us know your use case.

00:48:27.960 --> 00:48:29.370
I think that's
what we would love

00:48:29.370 --> 00:48:31.980
to know, just only with gVisor.

00:48:31.980 --> 00:48:35.550
But we wanted to make this
gVisor accessible to everybody

00:48:35.550 --> 00:48:37.994
through Kubernetes.

00:48:37.994 --> 00:48:40.410
We're leading the effort on
introducing the concept called

00:48:40.410 --> 00:48:44.460
sandbox pause specification
in the Kubernetes community

00:48:44.460 --> 00:48:45.330
right now.

00:48:45.330 --> 00:48:48.620
And of course, gVisor
is one with the runtimes

00:48:48.620 --> 00:48:52.350
that have been proposed for a
sandbox pause in Kubernetes.

00:48:52.350 --> 00:48:55.590
What it means is that,
well, Ian is a great hacker,

00:48:55.590 --> 00:48:57.570
so he can type in
a command nicely,

00:48:57.570 --> 00:49:01.270
but want to make
it more generic.

00:49:01.270 --> 00:49:04.730
Meaning, if we could
actually let people access

00:49:04.730 --> 00:49:07.080
use gVisor through
Kubernetes [INAUDIBLE],,

00:49:07.080 --> 00:49:08.410
how wonderful that could be.

00:49:08.410 --> 00:49:11.340
And I'm going to quickly
talk about what we're doing

00:49:11.340 --> 00:49:13.560
in the community as we speak.

00:49:13.560 --> 00:49:17.070
So sandboxing Kubernetes-- it's
not done yet, unfortunately.

00:49:17.070 --> 00:49:20.010
It would be great to finish up
and talk while, and yeah, we're

00:49:20.010 --> 00:49:23.320
done, but it's just a
work in progress really.

00:49:23.320 --> 00:49:25.740
Work in progress, but it
doesn't mean that we're

00:49:25.740 --> 00:49:27.750
making things inside Google.

00:49:27.750 --> 00:49:30.520
We're really doing just
everything in open source.

00:49:30.520 --> 00:49:33.060
So if you look at all the
open source discussion,

00:49:33.060 --> 00:49:36.510
you'll see Tim Allclair, our
tech lead for this project,

00:49:36.510 --> 00:49:38.550
and he's making huge progress.

00:49:38.550 --> 00:49:40.470
So the sandbox pods--

00:49:40.470 --> 00:49:44.430
so new Kubernetes API for the
sandbox containers were pods.

00:49:44.430 --> 00:49:46.447
It will be a
pod-level isolation.

00:49:46.447 --> 00:49:48.946
So for people who don't know
what the definition between pod

00:49:48.946 --> 00:49:50.737
and container-- well,
it's kind of similar.

00:49:50.737 --> 00:49:54.450
But pod is the
smallest granularity

00:49:54.450 --> 00:49:57.320
on the perimeter that could
be scheduled by Kubernetes.

00:49:57.320 --> 00:50:00.580
And inside a pod, you could
have multiple containers.

00:50:00.580 --> 00:50:03.190
So it will protect this
group of containers.

00:50:03.190 --> 00:50:05.580
That's a pod.

00:50:05.580 --> 00:50:09.540
We hope that for anything that's
going to be part of the sandbox

00:50:09.540 --> 00:50:13.400
pod we'll have at least two,
but multiple layers of isolation

00:50:13.400 --> 00:50:16.050
boundaries, because otherwise
it doesn't make so much sense

00:50:16.050 --> 00:50:17.700
to call this a sandbox pod.

00:50:17.700 --> 00:50:21.690
If you could break out,
what's the difference?

00:50:21.690 --> 00:50:24.190
This is the really
work-in-progress API.

00:50:24.190 --> 00:50:27.972
I have more detail, but I just
decided not to put too much.

00:50:27.972 --> 00:50:29.430
This is actually
the third version.

00:50:29.430 --> 00:50:30.480
When I started
working on the slide,

00:50:30.480 --> 00:50:32.188
this was the third
version I had changed,

00:50:32.188 --> 00:50:34.050
so as we speak
we're making change.

00:50:34.050 --> 00:50:36.360
But I think Tim
already has the very

00:50:36.360 --> 00:50:39.360
close to final form as
a submission, so we'll

00:50:39.360 --> 00:50:42.960
see how that lands in the
future version of Kubernetes.

00:50:42.960 --> 00:50:44.700
So just to kind of
give you a sneak peek,

00:50:44.700 --> 00:50:51.090
runtime class is a new API to
specify the runtime, meaning

00:50:51.090 --> 00:50:54.800
which runtime that I want to
use for my pod and containers.

00:50:54.800 --> 00:51:00.060
You can specify which runtime
class to use from the pod spec.

00:51:00.060 --> 00:51:03.570
So Tim emphasized
that, make it--

00:51:03.570 --> 00:51:04.980
V1 alpha is very clear.

00:51:04.980 --> 00:51:06.280
Yes, it's still working on it.

00:51:06.280 --> 00:51:08.405
So the name will be runtime
class, and in this case

00:51:08.405 --> 00:51:12.630
metadata name will be
gVisor, and a runtime handler

00:51:12.630 --> 00:51:15.780
will specify another gVisor,
what's another thing.

00:51:15.780 --> 00:51:17.310
And inside there,
it goes by which

00:51:17.310 --> 00:51:22.390
file of the runtime to use and
all the relevant specification.

00:51:22.390 --> 00:51:25.230
But that's something that
we believe that the cluster

00:51:25.230 --> 00:51:27.300
operator-- so if you're
running on premises,

00:51:27.300 --> 00:51:31.110
the cluster admin will be in
charge for setting that up.

00:51:31.110 --> 00:51:32.850
And in case of cloud,
it will probably

00:51:32.850 --> 00:51:35.070
be us, like cloud
operator providers

00:51:35.070 --> 00:51:38.770
will be configuring the left
side of the configuration.

00:51:38.770 --> 00:51:43.890
And all users have to do is to
write up a regular pod spec,

00:51:43.890 --> 00:51:46.980
specify in the spec that you're
specifying runtime class name,

00:51:46.980 --> 00:51:50.270
and choose whatever you want to.

00:51:50.270 --> 00:51:53.190
We talked about gVisor
today, but there are also

00:51:53.190 --> 00:51:55.270
other approaches, like
others containers.

00:51:55.270 --> 00:51:58.870
So we're working together to
standardize this specification.

00:51:58.870 --> 00:52:00.601
I think we're making
huge progress there.

00:52:03.490 --> 00:52:09.100
So today we talked about
the containerisation

00:52:09.100 --> 00:52:11.350
from the past and the present.

00:52:11.350 --> 00:52:16.180
The past is, like, iteration,
and the present is what we have

00:52:16.180 --> 00:52:18.504
open source-- what we
have in Kubernetes--

00:52:18.504 --> 00:52:19.420
Kubernetes and gVisor.

00:52:19.420 --> 00:52:22.570
And of course we just talked
a little bit of the future

00:52:22.570 --> 00:52:24.500
to bring it together.

00:52:24.500 --> 00:52:26.680
So if you're interested
in gVisor or running

00:52:26.680 --> 00:52:30.470
gVisor with Kubernetes
engine, please let us know.

00:52:30.470 --> 00:52:32.580
We would love to hear
your thoughts there.

00:52:32.580 --> 00:52:34.600
So this is a link to our forum.

00:52:34.600 --> 00:52:38.180
We'd love to continue the
discussion in a conversation.

00:52:38.180 --> 00:52:38.680
Thank you.

00:52:38.680 --> 00:52:40.840
We ran out of time, So hopefully
we could take questions

00:52:40.840 --> 00:52:41.460
